---
export const prerender = true

import DefaultLayout from '@layouts/DefaultLayout.astro'
---

<DefaultLayout title="About - Bishal Upadhyaya">
  <!--
    The photograph is the header.

    Before this, the page opened with a generic band titled "About Me" over a
    subtitle that said nothing, and then a snapshot boxed inside the 700px text
    column with a hairline around it. Three separate objects, two seams, and the
    picture — the only thing on the page that is actually about the person — was
    the smallest of them.

    Now there is one object. Two facts about the material decided the shape:

    1. The sky in this photograph is deep blue and --void is deep navy-black.
       They are neighbours. So the image can dissolve into the page instead of
       being framed by it, and the dissolve is nearly invisible where the sky
       meets the dark.

    2. The source is 880×586. Stretched edge to edge on a 1920px display it
       would be upscaled 2.2× and look it. Letting it occupy the right side of
       the viewport instead of the whole width keeps it near 1:1 — the crop is
       what makes it sharp AND what makes room for the words.

    So: he comes out of the dark on the right, the words sit in the dark on the
    left. No scrim over the type, which means no contrast gamble — the text is
    on genuine --void, and the image begins where the text ends.
  -->
  <header class="ab-hero">
    <div class="ab-media">
      <img
        src="/about-portrait.webp"
        alt="Bishal Upadhyaya on the south rim of the Grand Canyon in winter"
        width="880"
        height="586"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="container">
      <div class="ab-intro" data-reveal="settle">
        <p class="label-mono ab-eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1 class="ab-name">Bishal Upadhyaya.</h1>
        <p class="ab-lead">
          I started in neuroscience, recording electrical signals in living neural circuits. Now I build AI
          systems that ship. Research on one end, products people use on the other.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="about-content">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="content-wrapper">
        <div class="section" data-reveal="settle">
          <h2>What I do</h2>
          <p>
            Data pipelines, models, infrastructure, and the product on top. The bar is that it feels effortless
            to the person using it, not just impressive in a benchmark table.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Agents and LLM systems that survive production.</strong> Tool-calling with structured
              outputs, multi-model routing, evals in the loop, honest failure reporting. The demos on my home page
              run on these.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Retrieval and model efficiency.</strong> Agentic RAG, hybrid and semantic search over
              vector stores, and models distilled without giving up recall. The
              <a href="/notes/choon" class="inline-link">fingerprinting model</a> is 27.7M parameters doing work
              most teams throw 300M at.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Infrastructure a CTO can sign off on.</strong> Terraform-managed AWS and GCP,
              least-privilege credentials, SOC2-minded logging, cost governance with receipts.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section" data-reveal="settle">
          <h2>Current focus</h2>
          <p>
            I cofounded <a href="/#vibeset" class="inline-link">Vibeset Technologies</a>. Three products: Curation for
            setlists, Cue for music synced to picture, Choon for fingerprinting and provenance.
          </p>
          <p>
            Music is just where these systems ship today. The same spine went into early Alzheimer's detection at
            UCSF, and model-efficiency research at Thermo Fisher with NVIDIA and HPE. The domain changes. The rigour
            doesn't.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cta">
          <a href="/#contact" class="contact-button">Get in touch →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DefaultLayout>

<style lang="scss">
  /* ── The header ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .ab-hero {
    display: flex;
    position: relative;
    align-items: center;
    /* Capped in px as well as vh: past ~760px the 880px-wide source starts to
       show its resolution, and a header taller than the screen is a header the
       reader has to scroll past to reach the point. */
    min-height: clamp(480px, 68vh, 720px);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .ab-media {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 0 0 auto;
    width: min(64%, 1180px);

    img {
      display: block;
      /* 62% keeps his face above the vertical centre, clear of the lede. */
      object-position: 62% 38%;
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
      object-fit: cover;
    }

    /* The dissolve. Left edge goes first and goes furthest, because that is the
       side the words come from; the bottom follows it into the section below so
       there is no line between the header and the page. The top is barely
       touched — the sky is already almost --void up there, and fading what is
       already dark just makes it muddy. */
    mask-image:
      linear-gradient(to right, transparent, #000 46%),
      linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0%, #000 15%, #000 62%, transparent 97%);
    mask-composite: intersect;
    -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;
  }

  .ab-intro {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    max-width: 32rem;
  }

  .ab-eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-s);
    color: var(--paper-soft);
  }

  .ab-name {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-m);
    color: var(--paper);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5.4vw, 4rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .ab-lead {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 30rem;
    color: var(--paper-soft);
    font-size: clamp(1.02rem, 1.5vw, 1.2rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Below the fold the composition can't hold — a 32rem column and a photograph
     do not both fit. The image becomes a band the page opens with, still
     dissolving downward into the text, and the words follow it. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .ab-hero {
      display: block;
      min-height: 0;
      padding-bottom: var(--space-l);
    }

    .ab-media {
      position: relative;
      inset: auto;
      margin-bottom: var(--space-l);
      width: 100%;
      height: clamp(260px, 42vh, 380px);

      img {
        object-position: center 34%;
      }

      mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 52%, transparent 99%);
    }

    .ab-intro {
      max-width: none;
    }
  }

  /* ── The page ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .about-content {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 var(--space-3xl);
  }

  /* Left rag, not centred. The header's type starts at the container gutter,
     and a centred body column started 230px to the right of it on a wide
     screen — two left edges, neither of them deliberate. One column now. */
  .content-wrapper {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 700px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .content-wrapper {
      max-width: none;
    }
  }

  .section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);

    h2 {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
      color: var(--paper);
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.4vw, 1.9rem);
      line-height: 1.15;
      font-family: var(--font-heading);
      letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    }

    p {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
      color: var(--paper-soft);
      font-size: 1.1rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
    }

    ul {
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;

      li {
        position: relative;
        margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
        padding-left: 2rem;
        color: var(--paper-soft);
        font-size: 1.05rem;
        line-height: 1.7;

        /* The same signal rule that marks the phrases in the strip under the
           home page's hero — one mark for "here is a thing I do", used in both
           places. An arrow was doing a different job here: → means "leads to",
           and these three don't lead anywhere, they sit side by side. */
        &::before {
          position: absolute;
          top: 0.82em;
          left: 0;
          background: var(--grad-signal);
          width: 14px;
          height: 2px;
          content: '';
        }

        strong {
          color: var(--paper);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  .inline-link {
    color: var(--crimson-text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;

    &:hover {
      color: var(--crimson);
    }
  }

  .cta {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .contact-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    transition:
      transform 0.22s var(--ease-out),
      box-shadow 0.28s ease;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--crimson-deep);
    padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    color: var(--on-fill); /* not --paper: on paper that is the ink, 2.3:1 on the fill */
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;

    &:hover {
      transform: translateY(-1px);
      box-shadow: var(--glow-crimson);
    }
  }
</style>
